02Find the first differenceWhich numbers can we certify without generating each one?A baseline scans integers from 1 through n, converts each to a string, and checks whether every digit belongs to D. It is correct: it visits each candidate once and accepts exactly those using allowed digits.def baseline(digits, n):
    allowed = set(digits)
    count = 0
    for value in range(1, n + 1):
        valid = True
        for digit in str(value):
            if digit not in allowed:
                valid = False
                break
        if valid:
            count += 1
    return countLet L be the decimal length of n and m the number of allowed digits. With set membership, the baseline takes O(nL) worst-case time and O(L+m) temporary space. n can be a billion: constructing and checking every integer repeats too much work. Multiplication can replace enumeration.Group by length first. Every shorter number is smaller than n. For length k, each position has m independent choices, giving mᵏ numbers. For {1,3},31, the only shorter length is one: 2¹=2.Now consider equal lengths. A prefix is the fixed beginning; a suffix is the remaining digits. Write the two-digit numbers as 1_ or 3_. Since 1<3, both 11 and 13 are below 31, regardless of the last digit. Starting with 3 ties the bound, so the last digit matters: 1=1 continues, while 3>1 is excluded.In general, keep the earlier prefix equal to the bound. One smaller digit here permits mʳ suffixes, where r positions remain and m digits are available. With c smaller choices, add c×mʳ. An equal digit adds nothing yet: continue to the next position. A greater digit permits no valid suffix. When r=0, m⁰=1 counts the one empty suffix.Why is the count complete and disjoint? Any valid number is shorter or has the same length as n. For an equal-length number, its first differing digit must be smaller, and that position is unique. After each step, the counted blocks are shorter numbers and numbers whose first smaller position has been processed. Only the path matching the bound prefix remains undecided. If the current bound digit is absent from D, that path ends: return immediately. If all positions match, add 1 once for n itself.Two tempting shortcuts fail: adding all mᴸ words includes 33>31. Requiring every digit to be ≤ the corresponding bound digit rejects 13≤31 because 3>1 in the last position. Only the first difference decides!

02找到首个不同位这一步要回答：哪些整数可以不逐个生成，就确定全部合法？基线方法：从 1 检查到 n，把每个整数转成字符串，逐位检查是否属于 D。它正确，因为每个候选数恰好检查一次，且只接受所有数位都合法的数。def baseline(digits, n):
    allowed = set(digits)
    count = 0
    for value in range(1, n + 1):
        valid = True
        for digit in str(value):
            if digit not in allowed:
                valid = False
                break
        if valid:
            count += 1
    return count设 L 是 n 的十进制位数。用集合查询，基线最坏需要 O(nL) 时间、O(L+m) 临时空间，其中 m 是可用数字个数。n 可到十亿；重复构造和检查每个整数太昂贵。下面用乘法代替枚举。先按长度分组。比 n 短的数全部更小。长度为 k 时，每个位置都有 m 个独立选择，因此有 mᵏ 个数。对于 {1,3},31，较短的只有一位：2¹=2。再看同长度数。前缀就是开头已经固定的数位，后缀是剩下的数位。把两位数写成 1_ 或 3_：1<3，所以 11、13 都比 31 小，个位无需再比较。若第一位是 3，它和上界相等，才需要比较个位：1=1 可以继续，3>1 必须排除。一般地，已匹配的前缀固定后，当前位选一个比上界小的数字，剩余 r 位每位都有 m 个选择，因此增加 mʳ。若有 c 个较小数字，则增加 c×mʳ。当前位相等时不立即加数，只继续比较下一位；当前位更大时没有合法后缀。r=0 时 m⁰=1，代表一种空后缀。为什么不会重复或漏算？任何合法数要么更短，要么与 n 等长。在等长数中，找到首个不同位：它必须更小，而且这一个位置唯一。于是每一步之后，已计数的是较短数以及首个更小位置已处理的数；尚未决定的只剩与上界前缀完全相等的一条路径。若当前上界数字不在 D 中，这条路径消失，立即返回。若所有位置都相等，最后单独加 1，计入 n。两个诱惑：直接加 mᴸ 会把 33>31 也算进去；要求每一位都 ≤ 上界对应位则会漏掉 13≤31，因为个位 3>1。必须只看首个不同位！
